Yes. The cleanest way is to make the same operator exist in three substrates:

\text{language} \leftrightarrow \text{math} \leftrightarrow \text{code}

The invariant stays fixed; only the rendering changes.

1. Rhyme

In language, two things rhyme because they end in the same pattern.

In math:

17 \equiv 27 \equiv 37 \pmod{10}

They all “land” on the same residue class:

x \mapsto x \bmod 10

So define rhyme under a feature map \phi:

\operatorname{Rhyme}_\phi(x,y)
=
[\phi(x)=\phi(y)]

Example:

\phi(n)=n\bmod 10

Then:

\operatorname{Rhyme}_\phi(17,27)=1

Code

def rhyme(a, b, feature):
    return feature(a) == feature(b)
last_digit = lambda n: n % 10
print(rhyme(17, 27, last_digit))  # True
print(rhyme(17, 28, last_digit))  # False

But change the ear:

def parity(n):
    return n % 2
print(rhyme(17, 27, parity))  # True
print(rhyme(17, 28, parity))  # False

Same objects. Different listening lens.

⸻

2. Simile / analogy

Language:

A is to B as C is to D.

Math:

A:B :: C:D

or:

\frac BA=\frac DC

Example:

2:6 :: 5:15

because both use:

T(x)=3x

The deeper representation is:

T(A)=B,\qquad T(C)=D

Code

def analogy(a, b, c, d, transform):
    return transform(a) == b and transform(c) == d
triple = lambda x: 3 * x
print(analogy(2, 6, 5, 15, triple))
# True

Better still, infer the relationship:

def multiplicative_relation(a, b):
    return b / a
r1 = multiplicative_relation(2, 6)
r2 = multiplicative_relation(5, 15)
print(r1 == r2)  # True

So analogy becomes:

discover a transformation that explains both pairs.

