11. Code metaphor

This one matters for software architecture.

Consider:

for item in items:
    if valid(item):
        result.append(transform(item))

And:

[
    transform(item)
    for item in items
    if valid(item)
]

Surface form differs.

Same computational story:

\text{filter}
\rightarrow
\text{map}
\rightarrow
\text{collect}

That pattern itself can become an operator:

def filter_map(items, predicate, transform):
    return [
        transform(x)
        for x in items
        if predicate(x)
    ]

This is exactly the Numantic move:

\text{many instances}
\rightarrow
\text{shared invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{named operator}

Which is also what good software abstraction already does.

