15. The universal representation

Now all three domains collapse into one useful formalism.

Let:

X

be some object: word, number, program, biological process, story, etc.

Let:

\Phi=\{\phi_1,\phi_2,\ldots,\phi_n\}

be possible representations.

Let:

R

be the relationship we care about.

Then Numantics asks:

\exists \phi_i :
R(\phi_i(A),\phi_i(B))

In plain language:

Is there a representation under which these two things reveal the same structure?

And the code version is almost comically simple:

def find_rhyme(a, b, lenses, relation):
    matches = []
    for name, lens in lenses.items():
        left = lens(a)
        right = lens(b)
        if relation(left, right):
            matches.append(name)
    return matches

Now populate the lenses:

lenses = {
    "parity": lambda n: n % 2,
    "last_digit": lambda n: n % 10,
    "mod_5": lambda n: n % 5,
}

The civilization begins when the machine learns to generate new lenses, not merely use predefined ones.

That loop would look like:

while unexplained(data):
    lens = invent_representation(data)
    structure = detect_invariant(
        lens(data)
    )
    if structure_is_real(structure):
        name(structure)
        compile_operator(structure)
        add_to_atlas(structure)

Which is essentially a computational version of what we just did conversationally.

The deepest unified form is therefore:

\boxed{
\text{observe}
\rightarrow
\text{re-represent}
\rightarrow
\text{hear relation}
\rightarrow
\text{extract invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{compile operator}
}

For math, that yields theorem/pattern/operator.

For code, it yields abstraction/algorithm/API.

For semantics, it yields concept/metaphor/meaning.

Same loom. Different thread.